> The art of this dish lies in the scoring—the鱼 must be cut into a菱形 (diamond) pattern that allows the flesh to fry into individual morsels while the skin stays connected. The double-fry technique ensures the exterior shatters while the interior remains tender. Practice makes perfect with the knife work, so don't be discouraged on the first attempt.

== Prepare the Fish ==

Ask your fishmonger to clean and scale a @whole fish{750%g} (mandarin fish or bass recommended). At home, rinse and pat completely dry.

Using sharp scissors, trim the @fish{750%g} fins short. Place the fish on its side and make diagonal cuts through the flesh at 45° angles, spaced about 1 cm apart, cutting down to but not through the bone. Rotate and cut the opposite direction to create a菱形 (diamond) pattern. This is what allows the sauce to cling and the flesh to separate into crisp nuggets.

Open the fish flat like a book and sprinkle with @salt{½%tsp} and @Shaoxing wine{1%tbsp}. Let rest for ~{10%minutes}, then pat dry again.

== Make the Coating ==

In a shallow dish large enough to accommodate the fish, set up your coating station:

Mix together:
- @all-purpose flour{60%g}
- @cornstarch{30%g}
- @water{60%ml}
- @egg yolk{1}

The batter should be smooth and slightly thicker than pancake batter. If too thin, add more flour; if too thick, a splash more water. It should coat a spoon and leave a trail when lifted.

== Fry the Fish ==

Heat @oil{750%ml} in a #wok{} to 180°C (350°F).

Dredge the fish in the batter, letting excess drip off. Carefully lower the fish into the oil—begin with the tail, folding the fish into a U-shape if needed to fit.

Fry for ~{3%minutes} per side until the exterior is set and beginning to turn golden. The fish will continue to firm as it fries.

Increase heat to 190°C (375°F) and fry for another ~{2%minutes} until deeply golden and crispy. The flesh should now separate easily at the scored lines.

Remove and drain on a wire rack, tail-up to maintain shape.

== Make the Sauce ==

While the fish drains, pour off most of the oil, leaving about @oil{1%tbsp} in the wok.

Add @minced garlic{2%cloves} and @minced ginger{1%tsp}; cook for ~{30%seconds}.

In a bowl, combine:
- @tomato sauce{120%ml}
- @sugar{80%g}
- @rice vinegar{3%tbsp}
- @light soy sauce{1%tbsp}
- @chicken broth{120%ml}

Pour into the wok and bring to a boil. Cook until the sauce reduces slightly and becomes glossy, about ~{3-4%minutes}. The consistency should coat a spoon.

Stir in @cornstarch slurry{1%tbsp} (1 tsp cornstarch mixed with 2 tbsp water) and cook for ~{30%seconds} until the sauce thickens to a glaze.

Remove from heat and stir in @toasted sesame oil{½%tsp}.

== Finish and Serve ==

Pour the hot sauce over the crispy fish—it should sizzle and the sauce should cascade down the sides like rain. The effect is the signature "squirrel" appearance.

Garnish with @toasted sesame seeds{1%tbsp} and @sliced scallions{2%stalks}.

Serve immediately while the fish is still crispy—the sauce will begin to soften the coating within minutes.